# === Group B: boundaries already answered by a unicode script table =========
#
# isWideRune checks the four script tables (Han, Hangul, Katakana, Hiragana)
# and then nine hardcoded ranges. For these lines, the code point at the mutated
# boundary is ALREADY reported wide by a table, so whether the hardcoded check
# includes it or not cannot change the result. Measured, not assumed: the
# boundary code points below are all inside the table ranges.
#
# The tails that the tables do NOT cover are killed by
# TestToastManager_EveryWideRuneBoundaryIsExact, which is why only the
# table-covered ones remain here.

# internal/ui/toast.go:210 - `r >= 0x1100 && r <= 0x115F` (Hangul Jamo). The whole
# range is inside unicode.Hangul, so both boundary mutants are no-ops. This is
# the one range in the function that is genuinely redundant.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/ui/toast.go:210

# internal/ui/toast.go:213 and :216 - the head of CJK Radicals Supplement
# (0x2E80) and the tail of 0x3040-0x9FFF. U+2E80 and U+9FFF are both covered by
# a table, so widening or narrowing the check leaves the result unchanged. The
# tails these ranges exist for (U+2E9A, U+3040) are covered separately and their
# boundary mutants are killed.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/ui/toast.go:213
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/ui/toast.go:216

# internal/ui/toast.go:219 and :222 - the head of Hangul Syllables (0xAC00) and the
# head of CJK Compatibility Ideographs (0xF900). Both heads are already reported
# wide by unicode.Hangul and unicode.Han respectively.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/ui/toast.go:219
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/ui/toast.go:222

# internal/ui/toast.go:234 - the head of CJK Ext B (0x20000). U+20000 falls inside
# the table-covered part of the range; the code points only this check reaches
# (U+2A6E0 onward) have their own coverage in the boundary test.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/ui/toast.go:234

# === Group B2: internal/ui/bordered, clamp and inclusive-comparison edges ==
#
# All eleven are inclusive boundaries whose mutated branch is a no-op or
# unreachable. Verified one at a time by hand-applying each mutation and running
# the package suite, not inferred from the shape alone.
#
# 41    `if width < 2` -> `<= 2`: the floor only fires below 2, and at 2 the body
#       assigns the value already there.
# 73    `if innerWidth < 0` -> `<= 0`: the same clamp shape; at 0 the body
#       assigns 0.
# 93    `if targetContent < 1` -> `<= 1`: the same clamp shape.
# 96    `for len(contentLines) < targetContent` -> `<=`: at equality the body
#       appends one padding line, but the loop then re-tests with the same
#       length and exits, so the appended line is the only difference and it is
#       indistinguishable from the truncation at :100 firing on the same input.
# 100   `if len(contentLines) > targetContent` -> `>=`: the slice
#       `contentLines[:targetContent]` at equality is the full slice, so the
#       branch is a no-op.
# 147   `if titleWidth > innerWidth` -> `>=`: at equality the truncation
#       `ansi.Truncate(title, innerWidth, "")` is a no-op, because a title
#       already exactly the inner width survives it unchanged.
# 189   `if footerWidth > innerWidth` -> `>=`: same shape, same proof.
# 209   `if displayWidth <= innerWidth` -> `<`: at equality the pad path runs and
#       the wrap path would return the same single line, since a line already
#       the inner width cannot be shortened by a rune-per-chunk wrap.
# 220   `if padding < 0` -> `<= 0`: the clamp shape; at 0 the body assigns 0.
# 251   `if runes[i] == '\033' && i+1 < len(runes)` -> `i+1 <= len(runes)`: the
#       third conjunct indexes runes[i+1], so the guard must exclude equality.
#       At i+1 == len(runes) the second conjunct is true but the third indexes
#       past the end and panics, so the mutant cannot complete. The ARITHMETIC_BASE
#       sibling on the same expression (i+1 -> i+2) is stricter still, skipping a
#       byte of every sequence.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/ui/bordered/bordered.go:41
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/ui/bordered/bordered.go:73
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/ui/bordered/bordered.go:93
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/ui/bordered/bordered.go:96
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/ui/bordered/bordered.go:100
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/ui/bordered/bordered.go:147
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/ui/bordered/bordered.go:189
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/ui/bordered/bordered.go:209
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/ui/bordered/bordered.go:220
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/ui/bordered/bordered.go:251
ARITHMETIC_BASE internal/ui/bordered/bordered.go:251

# === Group B3: internal/app/txn.go, scan bounds in the SQL lexer ==========
#
# Eight survivors, all in maskNonCode's byte-level scanning. Every one was
# verified by hand-applying the exact mutation (span located by searching for the
# literal operator, not by the reported column) and running the package suite.
#
# The common shape: `i+1 < len(sql)` guards an access to sql[i+1]. Relaxing it to
# `i+1 <= len(sql)` fires the body at i+1 == len(sql), and the body either reads
# one byte past the end (a panic, so the mutant is not viable and reports
# LIVED-by-timeout) or advances i past the end, which ends the loop with i >= len
# and makes blank() clamp the same way. The counters are never wrong; they are
# just one index wider than the data they guard.
#
# 318:11 `if from < 0` -> `from <= 0`: blank()'s lower clamp. Every caller passes
#       a start index that is already >= 0 (it is a byte offset found by the
#       scan, or 0), so the `from = 0` body can only ever assign 0. At from == 0
#       the mutant fires and assigns the value already there.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/app/txn.go:318

# 321:9  `if to > len(out)` -> `to >= len(out)`: blank()'s upper clamp, the same
#        shape. At to == len(out) the body assigns the value already there, and
#        for the mutated call sites (an index one past the end) the write loop
#        `for i := from; i < to` simply does not execute, because the clamped
#        range is empty either way.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/app/txn.go:321

# 354:30 `if sql[i] == '\\' && i+1 < len(sql)` -> `i+1 <= len(sql)`: the E-string
#        backslash escape. A trailing backslash as the LAST byte of the input is
#        the only firing case; `i += 2` then leaves i == len+1, the loop exits,
#        and blank(start, i) clamps to the same end. No output byte differs.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/app/txn.go:354

# 396:11 `if i+1 < len(sql)` -> `i+1 <= len(sql)`: the block-comment terminator
#        check. Same shape: at i+1 == len(sql) the `i += 2` branch pushes i past
#        the end, and the following `else { i = len(sql) }` produces the same
#        final index that blank() would clamp to anyway.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/app/txn.go:396

# 338:32 / 374:32 `if i+1 < len(sql) && sql[i+1] == '\''` -> `i+2 < len(sql)`: the
#        doubled-quote escape in the single-quoted and double-quoted scanners.
#        ARITHMETIC_BASE retargets the ADDEND of the bound, not the operator. A
#        doubled quote is only meaningful at i+1; skipping to i+2 asks whether
#        the byte AFTER the candidate pair is a quote, which for every input is
#        either a different byte or out of range. When the pair is the last thing
#        in the input, both forms stop at the same end index.
ARITHMETIC_BASE internal/app/txn.go:338
ARITHMETIC_BASE internal/app/txn.go:374

# 354:27 `if sql[i] == '\\' && i+1 < len(sql)` -> `i+2 < len(sql)`: the same
#        retargeted addend in the E-string escape. A backslash escape needs the
#        escaped byte at i+1; asking about i+2 reads the byte after it.
ARITHMETIC_BASE internal/app/txn.go:354

# 396:8  `for i+1 < len(sql) && (...)` -> `i+2 < len(sql)`: the block-comment
#        body scan. The retargeted addend stops the scan one byte early, but the
#        only thing the loop finds is the `*/` terminator, and the trailing
#        `if i+1 < len(sql)` re-checks the bound before consuming it, so an early
#        stop lands on the same end index.
ARITHMETIC_BASE internal/app/txn.go:396

# === Group B4: internal/ui/components/grid/mouse.go, viewport arithmetic ====
#
# FOUR survivors, not three. I probed `acc+w > available` as a relaxation to
# `>=`, concluded it survived, and wrote that into this file. The full run
# disagreed and killed it. Line 68 IS that break and it IS killable, because a
# column ending exactly at the content width is selectable and
# TestVisibleColumnAtX_ColumnFittingExactlyIsSelectable pins that. What I probed
# was line 71, the `contentX < acc+w` test, one line down.
#
# That is the second time a hand-applied probe has disagreed with the tool, and
# both times the PROBE was wrong: once because it applied an invalid span, once
# because it targeted the wrong line. Trust `make mutate` over a probe, and
# never add an allowlist entry from a probe alone.
#
# The remaining four are verified by hand-applying the exact mutation (span
# located by searching the line for the literal) and running the package suite.
# Each is an inclusive edge whose mutated branch is unreachable or a no-op.
#
# The other 41 mutants in this file are killed by mouse_test.go, including the
# arithmetic these four sit next to: available = width-2
# (AvailableIsWidthMinusBothBorders), the exclusive right edge of a column
# (ColumnRightEdgeIsExclusive), and vj >= 0 accepting the first column
# (FirstVisibleColumnIsReachable). The arithmetic around them is pinned; only
# these boundaries are not distinguishable.

# mouse.go:61 `if available <= 0` -> `available < 0`: available is width-2, so
# this is the "no content area" guard. The mutant lets available == 0 (width 2)
# into the loop, where `acc+w > 0` breaks immediately for any positive width and
# returns -1, the same answer. A zero-width first column does not break there,
# but `contentX < 0 + 0` is false for every contentX and the next iteration
# breaks anyway. The function returns -1 for every x either way.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/ui/components/grid/mouse.go:61

# mouse.go:66 both loop bounds, `i < len(mh.grid.columns)` and
# `i < len(mh.grid.widths)`, -> `<=`: the loop indexes mh.grid.widths[i] on the
# next line, so at i == len(...) the mutant reads out of range and panics. The
# mutated run cannot complete, which gremlins reports as LIVED rather than NOT
# VIABLE because the panic happens in a goroutine it does not join.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/ui/components/grid/mouse.go:66

# mouse.go:28 `if y < headerY` -> `y <= headerY`: HandleClick tests `y ==
# headerY` first and returns there, so by the time this branch runs y !=
# headerY. An inclusive `<=` therefore reduces to the strict `<`, and the
# mutated branch is unreachable. This is why
# TestGrid_HandleClick_LineAboveTheHeaderIsNotTheHeader kills nothing here: no
# input can reach the difference.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/ui/components/grid/mouse.go:28

# === Group C: one accepted, documented risk ================================
#
# internal/ui/toast.go:78 - `now.Sub(toast.Created) < toast.Duration` -> `<=`.
#
# This is NOT provably equivalent: at exactly toast.Duration elapsed, the mutant
# keeps a toast the original expires. It is accepted rather than equivalent
# because the boundary is unreachable without controlling the clock: the test
# would have to make elapsed time exactly equal the duration, which means
# sleeping for a precisely-known duration and racing the scheduler. Every
# measurable duration is either clearly under or clearly over.
#
# Accepted because the real-world effect is nil: Duration is set to 3s by Show()
# and the risk window is a single instant at the 3.000s mark, where keeping a
# toast for one more frame is not a defect. The clock-dependent behaviour itself
# IS covered by TestToastManager_ExpiryComparisonIsStrict, which kills the
# negation variant.
CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY internal/ui/toast.go:78
